<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Wied-Neuwied</h1><p class="st-meta">1698 CE – 1806 CE · capital Neuwied</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rhineland county whose capital, Neuwied, was founded as a haven of religious tolerance and became famous for fine furniture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-wied">Frederick III of Wied</a> founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1653, inviting settlers with promises of religious freedom. In 1698 the Wied lands were divided between Wied-Runkel and Wied-Neuwied, the latter ruling from the new town.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a>, Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Reformed Christians, each with its own congregation. The cabinetmakers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-roentgen">Abraham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-roentgen">David Roentgen</a> built a workshop there whose marquetry furniture was sold to the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St Petersburg</a>. The counts were raised to princes in 1784. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-wied-neuwied">Prince Maximilian of Wied-Neuwied</a> later became known for expeditions to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wied-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> was mediatised in 1806 and placed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a>, then in 1815 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>. The princely family kept its palace in Neuwied.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1698 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.49%;width:2.15%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1698 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neuwied</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick III, Johann Friedrich Alexander</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Wied-Neuwied, Wied</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=county-of-wied-neuwied" aria-label="County of Wied-Neuwied on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 426 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Wied-Neuwied lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5170 682L5229 694M5170 682L5189 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5189 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Neuwied. <a href="/atlas?year=1752&amp;focus=county-of-wied-neuwied">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
